{"id":"83b87f67-769b-4718-9bea-e08f3b20951e","object":"chat.completion","created":1785028220,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # Extract data\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    \\n    # Build node dict and parent-child relationships\\n    node_dict = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        node_dict[n['id']] = n\\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in node_dict}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\\n    \\n    # Precompute all descendants for each node (used repeatedly)\\n    descendants = {}\\n    def get_descendants(nid):\\n        if nid in descendants:\\n            return descendants[nid]\\n        res = set()\\n        for c in children.get(nid, []):\\n            res.add(c)\\n            res.update(get_descendants(c))\\n        descendants[nid] = res\\n        return res\\n    for nid in node_dict:\\n        get_descendants(nid)\\n    \\n    # Sort candidates: all nodes except root, by depth descending, then ID ascending\\n    candidates = []\\n    for nid, n in node_dict.items():\\n        if nid == 'root':  # root never removable\\n            continue\\n        candidates.append(nid)\\n    # Depth descending, then ID ascending (lexicographic)\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda nid: (-node_dict[nid]['depth'], nid))\\n    \\n    removed = set()\\n    removed_order = []\\n    \\n    # Helper to compute set of symbols currently present (nodes not removed)\\n    def current_symbols():\\n        syms = set()\\n        for nid, n in node_dict.items():\\n            if nid not in removed:\\n                syms.add(n['symbol'])\\n        return syms\\n    \\n    for nid in candidates:\\n        if nid in removed:\\n            continue\\n        n = node_dict[nid]\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        # Nodes that would be removed: this node + all descendants not already removed\\n        to_remove = {nid}\\n        to_remove.update(descendants[nid])\\n        to_remove = to_remove - removed  # already removed nodes are not considered\\n        # Check all nodes in to_remove are removable\\n        all_removable = all(node_dict[rid]['removable'] for rid in to_remove)\\n        if not all_removable:\\n            continue\\n        # Check required symbols still present after removal\\n        retained_symbols = current_symbols()\\n        for rid in to_remove:\\n            retained_symbols.discard(node_dict[rid]['symbol'])\\n        if required_symbols.issubset(retained_symbols):\\n            # Perform removal\\n            removed.update(to_remove)\\n            removed_order.append(nid)\\n    \\n    # Retained nodes: all not removed, sorted by ID\\n    retained_ids = sorted([nid for nid in node_dict if nid not in removed])\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_ids\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for SE-PE-01 based on the description: \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\". The payload contains a tree of nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols list. The objective is to reduce the tree by removing removable nodes while preserving that all required symbols remain represented. Also must keep root and nodes with retained children. Deeper nodes before shallower nodes. Tentatively remove only if all required symbols still present. Use node ID as tie break at equal depth. Return sorted retained node IDs and the actual removal order (i.e., removed_node_ids in order of removal, retained_node_ids sorted? The schema says removed_node_ids is an array of strings, retained_node_ids is an array of strings. No explicit sort order mentioned except for retained \"sorted retained IDs\"? The candidate_artifact says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted (presumably ascending, lexicographically? But IDs are like \"root\", \"n1\", etc. Probably lexicographically). removed_node_ids should be in the order they were removed (the actual removal order). \n\nWe need to implement a reduction algorithm. Given tree, we want to remove as many removable nodes as possible? Or select a subset? The description says \"tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" This sounds like a greedy algorithm: consider nodes in some order (by depth descending, then by ID ascending?), and if removable, and if removing it (and possibly its descendants) still leaves all required symbols present, then remove it. But careful: removing a node also removes its subtree? In parse tree reduction, typically you can delete a node and replace it with its subtree? But the description says \"grammar_valid\" but not much about replacement. However the payload includes \"parent\" and \"depth\", and \"removable\" flag. I think the operation is to delete the node from the tree, meaning its children become children of its parent (or removed too?). Actually typical Perses reduction: you can delete a node and its entire subtree if it's removable and the resulting tree still has all required symbols. Or you can replace a node with its child. But here the data only describes tree structure, no child list. The tree is given as list of nodes with parent references. So we can build parent-child relationships. The description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That suggests we cannot remove a node if it has children that are not removed (i.e., retained children). So removing a node implies removing its entire subtree? But if a node's children are also removed, then it's okay. However \"node with retained children\" means that if we try to remove a node, we must first decide whether its children will be removed as well. So the algorithm likely: For each node in order (deep first), if it is removable, and if removing it (and all its descendants) still preserves required symbols, then mark it for removal. After removal, its descendants are also removed. The tree is being reduced. But we must also consider that removing a node might make some required symbols absent, but those symbols might be present elsewhere. So we need to compute the set of symbols after removal.\n\nSimplify: Given the tree, we want to find a set of removable nodes to remove such that:\n- root is never removed.\n- No node with required symbol is removed.\n- If a node is removed, all its descendants are also removed (since otherwise it would have retained children, which is forbidden).\n- The resulting tree contains all required symbols (i.e., the set of symbols of nodes that are not removed must include the required_symbols list).\n- We want to maximize removals? Or just greedy? The description says \"tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That suggests we consider nodes in order (depth descending, then ID ascending) and if removable AND removing it doesn't cause loss of any required symbol, then we remove it. After removal, we continue with remaining nodes. That is a greedy algorithm. The \"registered_candidate_order\" probably refers to this order. And \"stable_ties\" using node ID.\n\nThe resultant removed_node_ids are the order of removal (the sequence of nodes we removed). retained_node_ids are the IDs of nodes that were not removed, sorted (presumably lexicographically or maybe by ID? We'll sort them in ascending order). The schema expects array of strings. Sorting consistently.\n\nEdge cases: What about nodes that are not removable? They stay. But if a node is not removable but its parent is removed, then it would become child of grandparent? But the operation says removing a node removes its subtree, so non-removable children can't be removed if parent is removed? Actually \"node with retained children\" prevents removing parent if any child is retained. So if a node has children that are not removed, we cannot remove the parent. So algorithm: we process nodes from deepest to shallowest. For each node, if it's removable and its required symbol condition holds, we remove it (meaning we mark it and all its descendants removed). But descendants might have been considered earlier (deeper nodes) and could have been removed already. So we need to manage state.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Build parent-child relationships. Create a dict from id to node object. Also a list of children for each node.\n2. Determine initial set of required symbols: required_symbols list.\n3. Compute initial symbol coverage: set of symbols of all nodes.\n4. We'll maintain a set of removed node IDs, initially empty.\n5. We'll also maintain a remap of parents? Since removal of a node effectively removes its subtree, we might not need to update tree structure because we only care about symbol coverage. So we can treat removal as marking node and all its descendants as removed.\n6. Create a list of candidate nodes to consider: all nodes except root, which is never removable. Also exclude nodes with required symbols? Actually nodes can have required symbols, and removable flag may be false for them anyway. But if a node has required symbol and is removable (unlikely but possible?), we must not remove it because required symbols must remain. So we should exclude nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols? But the payload shows root has symbol \"root\" which is required, and root is not removable. Also some nodes have symbol \"s1\" which is required, and they are not removable in the examples. In example, n1 has symbol \"s1\" and removable false. n4 has symbol \"s1\" and removable true. So required symbols can appear in removable nodes. Therefore we cannot remove any node that would cause loss of a required symbol. So we need to check symbol coverage after removal.\n7. Sort candidates by depth descending, then by ID ascending (since ID is string, we can compare directly). But note: depth descending means deeper first; for same depth, tie-break by ID ascending (lexicographic).\n8. Process each candidate in that order. For each node, if it's already removed (because its ancestor was removed earlier), skip.\n   Check if it's removable. Also, we need to check if removing it (and all its descendants that are not already removed) would cause any required symbol to become absent. Since we process deepest first, any descendant that could have been removed is already considered; but if we remove this node, all its descendants (including those not yet removed) become removed too. So we need to know the set of symbols that would be lost: symbols of the node itself and all its descendants that are currently not removed. But descendants might have been removed already if they were processed earlier? Actually since we process deepest first, descendants are processed before ancestors. So if a descendant was removable, it would have been removed unless it would violate required symbols. But if it was removable but not removed because removing it would lose required symbol, then it is retained. Then when we consider the ancestor, if we remove the ancestor, we would also remove that descendant (which has a required symbol), causing loss. So we need to check the entire subtree rooted at this node, ignoring nodes already removed. We'll compute the set of symbols in the subtree that are not already removed. If that set contains any required symbol that is not present elsewhere in the tree (outside the subtree) and also not already removed, then removal would lose that required symbol. But careful: a required symbol might be present both inside and outside the subtree. So we need to check if the removal would make the remaining tree missing that required symbol. That is: for each required symbol, after removal, is there at least one node with that symbol not removed? So we need to compute the overall symbol frequency after removal, maybe by counting occurrences. Simpler: precompute counts of each symbol in the whole tree. Then for each candidate node, compute the counts of symbols in its subtree (that are not already removed). If for any required symbol, the count in subtree equals the total count in the entire tree (i.e., all occurrences are in that subtree), then removal would lose it. But we also need to consider that some occurrences might have been removed already by earlier steps? Since we process descendants first, when checking ancestor, the descendant removals already happened, so the subtree counts should only include nodes not removed. So we need to dynamically maintain counts.\n   Alternative: simulate removal by temporarily assuming we remove the node and all its descendants that are not already removed, then check if all required symbols remain. Since the tree is small (fixtures have 7 nodes), we can just compute the set of retained symbols after removal. But we must ensure we don't remove nodes that have already been removed. We'll maintain a set `removed`. For each candidate, if it's not in removed, we compute the set of nodes that would be removed if we remove this node: this node plus all its descendants that are not already in removed. Then compute new retained symbols = current retained symbols minus the symbols of those nodes. Check if all required symbols are still present. If yes, we add all those nodes to `removed` (removed list order: the node itself first? The actual removal order likely is the order we process the node, i.e., the parent removal order. So we append the node's ID to removed_node_ids list when we decide to remove it. Also we mark all its descendants as removed, but those descendants might have been removed earlier or later? Since we process deeper first, descendants would have been considered earlier and either removed or not. If a descendant was not removed because it would lose required, then now removing the ancestor would also remove that descendant, which might lose required. So we handle that by the check. So order: we process candidates in order, when we decide to remove a node, we remove the entire subtree (including any previously retained nodes). That means some nodes that were retained (because they weren't removable or were but we didn't remove due to requirement) might now be removed. That's okay because the ancestor removal is a later step.\n\n   Implementation: Build tree with parent references. For each node, we need list of descendants. Could precompute subtree node ids. But since dynamic deletions, we can compute on the fly by traversing. Since small, we can just traverse each time.\n\n9. After processing all candidates, we have a set removed_nodes (including all nodes removed either directly or as descendants). The retained nodes are all nodes not in removed. Sort retained node IDs (lexicographically). removed_node_ids list is the order we appended (the direct removal decisions). We need to output both.\n\nBut note: The \"actual removal order\" might be the order of nodes whose removal was decided, not the order of all nodes removed (including descendants). So we only record the node ID when we explicitly decide to remove it (the candidate node). That matches the sequence of decisions.\n\nAlso check: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" We enforce through checks: root is not in candidates; required symbol node removal is prevented by requirement check; node with retained children: if a node has children that are not in removed set, then we would be removing it while retaining children, which we should not do. Our algorithm removes entire subtree, so this is automatically satisfied because removal includes children. But if a node is removable and we decide to remove it, we also remove all children. So no retained children.\n\nEdge case: What if a node is removable but has children that are not removable? In the payload, children have removable flag. If a child is not removable, then removing the parent would also remove that child, which might violate required symbols or something. But if the child is not removable, we might still remove it if the parent removal is allowed. The rule \"node with retained children\" means we cannot remove a node if any of its children are retained. But if we remove the parent, we also remove the children, so they are not retained. So it's allowed to remove a parent that has non-removable children, as long as the children get removed. However the children might be non-removable? The flag \"removable\" indicates if they can be removed individually. But they can be removed as part of parent removal? Usually in Perses, only removable nodes can be deleted; non-removable nodes cannot be deleted even as part of deletion of parent. But the description: \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That hints that children could be retained if they are not removed. So if a child is not removable, you cannot remove it; thus the parent cannot be removed because it would have a retained child? Actually if the child is not removable, you cannot remove the child, so the child would be retained. So the parent cannot be removed. So in our algorithm, we are removing the entire subtree when we remove a node. That includes non-removable nodes, which might be against the rules. The problem statement says \"removable\" flag. So only nodes with removable true can be removed. But when we remove a parent, we are effectively removing all descendants, regardless of their removable flag. But is that allowed? Typically in Perses, only removable nodes can be deleted; non-removable nodes are fixed. So if a parent is removable, you can choose to delete it, but its children (even if non-removable) would be deleted too? That would violate the rule that non-removable nodes cannot be removed. So likely you cannot delete a node if it would